1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,492
The night of February 13th, 1951.

2
00:00:03,512 --> 00:00:06,529
Chipyong-ni, Gyeonggi Province.

3
00:00:06,685 --> 00:00:08,851
Inside a small, snow-covered basin,

4
00:00:08,871 --> 00:00:13,038
about 5,600 American and French troops were trapped.

5
00:00:13,454 --> 00:00:14,790
Every hill around them was

6
00:00:14,810 --> 00:00:16,644
packed with Chinese troops.

7
00:00:16,666 --> 00:00:19,286
There was no way out. Chipyong-ni was

8
00:00:19,306 --> 00:00:21,794
an island, completely surrounded.

9
00:00:22,233 --> 00:00:24,447
The Chinese command was certain.

10
00:00:24,515 --> 00:00:25,963
Once again, they believed,

11
00:00:25,983 --> 00:00:28,579
the UN forces would break and run.

12
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,620
For nearly three months since

13
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:32,510
China entered the war, the UN

14
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:34,389
had done nothing but retreat.

15
00:00:34,827 --> 00:00:36,602
The surrounded force: the U.S.

16
00:00:36,622 --> 00:00:39,743
23rd Infantry Regiment and the French Battalion.

17
00:00:39,911 --> 00:00:41,724
Against them came several Chinese

18
00:00:41,744 --> 00:00:44,172
divisions, many times their number.

19
00:00:44,748 --> 00:00:46,051
Yet three days later,

20
00:00:46,071 --> 00:00:49,386
it was the Chinese who pulled back first.

21
00:00:49,817 --> 00:00:51,496
The place where China's human-wave

22
00:00:51,516 --> 00:00:54,182
tactics were first broken head-on.

23
00:00:54,282 --> 00:00:56,516
This is the story of the 'Gettysburg of the

24
00:00:56,536 --> 00:00:59,938
Korean War': the Battle of Chipyong-ni.

25
00:01:00,372 --> 00:01:03,839
In October 1950, China entered the war.

26
00:01:04,064 --> 00:01:06,685
UN forces lost Seoul again and fell

27
00:01:06,705 --> 00:01:09,352
back as far as Pyeongtaek and Wonju.

28
00:01:09,804 --> 00:01:13,031
On January 4th, 1951, Seoul fell

29
00:01:13,051 --> 00:01:15,058
once more, in what Koreans

30
00:01:15,078 --> 00:01:17,754
call the January 4th Retreat.

31
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,256
Among the troops spread a fear that the

32
00:01:20,276 --> 00:01:22,841
Chinese simply could not be beaten.

33
00:01:23,258 --> 00:01:25,838
Then came Lieutenant General Matthew Ridgway,

34
00:01:25,858 --> 00:01:28,330
the Eighth Army's new commander.

35
00:01:28,414 --> 00:01:29,896
He resolved to stop retreating

36
00:01:29,916 --> 00:01:31,815
and meet the Chinese head-on.

37
00:01:32,246 --> 00:01:35,782
On February 11th, 1951, the Chinese

38
00:01:35,802 --> 00:01:38,149
launched another major offensive.

39
00:01:38,267 --> 00:01:40,853
As the line near Hoengseong collapsed,

40
00:01:40,873 --> 00:01:44,267
the Chipyong-ni force was left jutting out alone.

41
00:01:44,708 --> 00:01:47,609
Chipyong-ni was a crossroads to Yangpyeong,

42
00:01:47,629 --> 00:01:51,102
Yeoju, and Wonju, on the Jungang rail line.

43
00:01:51,157 --> 00:01:52,947
Lose it, and the entire central

44
00:01:52,967 --> 00:01:54,599
front could start to crumble.

45
00:01:55,054 --> 00:01:57,591
But the terrain was as bad as it gets.

46
00:01:57,701 --> 00:01:59,354
The village sat in a basin about

47
00:01:59,374 --> 00:02:01,358
100 meters above sea level,

48
00:02:01,378 --> 00:02:05,013
ringed by hills of 300 to 400 meters.

49
00:02:05,452 --> 00:02:07,587
On the morning of February 13th,

50
00:02:07,607 --> 00:02:10,010
the corps commander ordered a withdrawal.

51
00:02:10,065 --> 00:02:13,346
But that same day, Ridgway overruled him.

52
00:02:13,366 --> 00:02:15,732
Chipyong-ni would be held.

53
00:02:16,163 --> 00:02:18,255
In early February, this regiment had

54
00:02:18,275 --> 00:02:20,235
already fought the Chinese near the Twin

55
00:02:20,255 --> 00:02:22,705
Tunnels, southeast of Chipyong-ni.

56
00:02:22,730 --> 00:02:24,258
The survivors of that fight now

57
00:02:24,278 --> 00:02:26,512
dug in again at Chipyong-ni.

58
00:02:26,953 --> 00:02:28,195
The regimental commander,

59
00:02:28,215 --> 00:02:30,948
Colonel Paul Freeman, made his call.

60
00:02:31,017 --> 00:02:32,659
Without the men to hold the high

61
00:02:32,679 --> 00:02:34,845
ridges, he built a tight ring on

62
00:02:34,865 --> 00:02:37,013
the low hills around the village.

63
00:02:37,456 --> 00:02:38,799
A circular perimeter,

64
00:02:38,819 --> 00:02:40,994
a little over a mile across.

65
00:02:41,169 --> 00:02:42,924
1st Battalion took the north,

66
00:02:42,944 --> 00:02:45,201
3rd the east, 2nd the south,

67
00:02:45,221 --> 00:02:47,281
and the French Battalion the west.

68
00:02:47,698 --> 00:02:49,597
The French Battalion holding the west

69
00:02:49,617 --> 00:02:52,760
was led by Lieutenant Colonel Monclar.

70
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:54,526
A three-star general who demoted

71
00:02:54,546 --> 00:02:55,893
himself to lead a battalion

72
00:02:55,913 --> 00:02:58,824
in Korea, he was 59 years old.

73
00:02:59,245 --> 00:03:01,467
A veteran of both World Wars, he had

74
00:03:01,487 --> 00:03:04,440
about 800 French soldiers at his side.

75
00:03:04,863 --> 00:03:07,229
February 13th, 10 p.m.

76
00:03:07,269 --> 00:03:09,126
Out of the darkness came the sound

77
00:03:09,146 --> 00:03:11,551
of bugles, whistles, and gongs.

78
00:03:11,996 --> 00:03:14,181
The Chinese struck from the northwest,

79
00:03:14,201 --> 00:03:16,605
the north, and the southeast at once.

80
00:03:16,773 --> 00:03:18,404
Soon, the perimeter was under

81
00:03:18,424 --> 00:03:20,026
attack from every direction.

82
00:03:20,435 --> 00:03:21,749
When the front rank fell,

83
00:03:21,769 --> 00:03:24,344
another surged forward behind it.

84
00:03:24,401 --> 00:03:25,713
This was China's so-called

85
00:03:25,733 --> 00:03:27,352
human-wave assault.

86
00:03:27,777 --> 00:03:29,184
The artillery at the center of

87
00:03:29,204 --> 00:03:32,177
the ring fired without pause.

88
00:03:32,297 --> 00:03:33,996
Inside a surrounded circle,

89
00:03:34,016 --> 00:03:36,609
every direction was the front line.

90
00:03:37,051 --> 00:03:39,001
On the night of February 14th,

91
00:03:39,021 --> 00:03:42,186
the Chinese came back in even greater numbers.

92
00:03:42,355 --> 00:03:44,122
Starting near midnight, they hammered

93
00:03:44,142 --> 00:03:46,880
the north, then the east, then the west.

94
00:03:47,319 --> 00:03:50,643
On February 14th, Colonel Freeman himself

95
00:03:50,663 --> 00:03:53,442
was hit in the leg by mortar fragments.

96
00:03:53,611 --> 00:03:55,470
Still, he limped from position to

97
00:03:55,490 --> 00:03:58,223
position and never stopped commanding.

98
00:03:58,658 --> 00:04:01,533
Ammunition began to run dangerously low.

99
00:04:01,588 --> 00:04:03,492
Overhead, transport planes dropped

100
00:04:03,512 --> 00:04:05,585
ammunition crates by parachute.

101
00:04:05,754 --> 00:04:07,806
Some men who left their foxholes to grab

102
00:04:07,826 --> 00:04:10,136
the crates were cut down by snipers.

103
00:04:10,571 --> 00:04:12,928
The most famous moment came in the west,

104
00:04:12,948 --> 00:04:15,227
on the French Battalion's line.

105
00:04:15,395 --> 00:04:17,674
Answering the Chinese bugles, the French

106
00:04:17,694 --> 00:04:21,916
sounded sirens, fixed bayonets, and charged.

107
00:04:22,353 --> 00:04:24,897
Faced with strange sounds and charging

108
00:04:24,917 --> 00:04:28,095
bayonets, the Chinese ranks scattered.

109
00:04:28,234 --> 00:04:29,830
After hand-to-hand fighting,

110
00:04:29,850 --> 00:04:32,368
the western line held to the end.

111
00:04:32,804 --> 00:04:35,949
That night, Company I of the 3rd Battalion

112
00:04:35,969 --> 00:04:38,144
was the first position to be breached,

113
00:04:38,165 --> 00:04:39,727
but its men counterattacked

114
00:04:39,747 --> 00:04:41,650
and retook it at once.

115
00:04:42,229 --> 00:04:44,722
Dawn, February 15th.

116
00:04:44,778 --> 00:04:46,522
At last, in the south,

117
00:04:46,542 --> 00:04:49,092
Company G's position collapsed.

118
00:04:49,518 --> 00:04:52,326
The Chinese poured down from Hill 397

119
00:04:52,346 --> 00:04:54,488
and drove into the southern perimeter.

120
00:04:54,539 --> 00:04:56,578
The Ranger company counterattacked,

121
00:04:56,598 --> 00:04:58,264
but was thrown back after

122
00:04:58,284 --> 00:05:00,364
brutal hand-to-hand combat.

123
00:05:00,779 --> 00:05:02,827
Private Bruno Orig of Company G

124
00:05:02,847 --> 00:05:05,103
stayed alone on his machine gun

125
00:05:05,123 --> 00:05:07,902
until his comrades got clear.

126
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:09,753
He was killed where he stood,

127
00:05:09,773 --> 00:05:12,478
and later received the Medal of Honor.

128
00:05:12,875 --> 00:05:15,586
Sergeant Sitman of Company M threw himself

129
00:05:15,606 --> 00:05:18,477
on a grenade, saving five of his men.

130
00:05:18,601 --> 00:05:20,333
At Chipyong-ni alone,

131
00:05:20,353 --> 00:05:22,880
two soldiers earned the Medal of Honor.

132
00:05:23,325 --> 00:05:25,572
That afternoon, a relief force

133
00:05:25,592 --> 00:05:27,870
set out from Yeoju to the south.

134
00:05:27,986 --> 00:05:30,027
It was an armored column of 23

135
00:05:30,047 --> 00:05:32,064
tanks under Colonel Crombez,

136
00:05:32,084 --> 00:05:34,707
commander of the 5th Cavalry Regiment.

137
00:05:35,134 --> 00:05:36,267
Clinging to the tanks were

138
00:05:36,287 --> 00:05:38,702
some 160 infantrymen.

139
00:05:38,809 --> 00:05:41,863
As they passed through Koksu-ri, gunfire

140
00:05:41,883 --> 00:05:44,784
rained down from the hills on both sides.

141
00:05:45,219 --> 00:05:46,663
The tanks kept rolling without

142
00:05:46,683 --> 00:05:48,768
stopping, and many infantrymen

143
00:05:48,788 --> 00:05:50,930
were left behind on the road.

144
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:53,241
When the column reached Chipyong-ni,

145
00:05:53,261 --> 00:05:56,272
only 23 riders were still aboard.

146
00:05:56,714 --> 00:05:59,268
Around 5 p.m., the tanks finally

147
00:05:59,288 --> 00:06:01,543
rolled into the Chipyong-ni perimeter.

148
00:06:01,571 --> 00:06:03,475
With the ring broken, the Chinese

149
00:06:03,495 --> 00:06:05,556
began withdrawing to the north.

150
00:06:05,989 --> 00:06:08,419
In the clearing skies, fighter-bombers

151
00:06:08,439 --> 00:06:10,707
hammered the encircling forces,

152
00:06:10,727 --> 00:06:13,115
and the three-day siege was over.

153
00:06:13,566 --> 00:06:15,721
How did some 5,000 surrounded men hold

154
00:06:15,741 --> 00:06:18,225
off an enemy many times their size?

155
00:06:18,331 --> 00:06:20,409
Let's break it down into four factors.

156
00:06:20,829 --> 00:06:22,873
First, the circular perimeter.

157
00:06:23,312 --> 00:06:25,536
Because the ring was small and tight,

158
00:06:25,556 --> 00:06:28,665
reserves inside could rush to any breach.

159
00:06:28,730 --> 00:06:30,679
And from the center, the artillery

160
00:06:30,699 --> 00:06:33,637
could swing its guns in any direction.

161
00:06:34,050 --> 00:06:36,632
Second, firepower and the sky.

162
00:06:37,082 --> 00:06:38,467
Transports made twenty-four

163
00:06:38,487 --> 00:06:39,810
ammunition drops,

164
00:06:39,830 --> 00:06:41,369
and once the skies cleared,

165
00:06:41,389 --> 00:06:44,103
fighter-bombers pounded the ring of attackers.

166
00:06:44,528 --> 00:06:47,700
Third, leaders who refused to fall back.

167
00:06:48,134 --> 00:06:50,485
Ridgway chose to hold instead of retreat,

168
00:06:50,505 --> 00:06:53,012
while the wounded Freeman and 59-year-old

169
00:06:53,032 --> 00:06:55,922
Monclar fought beside their men.

170
00:06:56,364 --> 00:06:58,373
Fourth, the armored relief.

171
00:06:58,795 --> 00:07:00,506
Crombez's tanks tore through the

172
00:07:00,526 --> 00:07:02,395
encirclement without stopping.

173
00:07:02,486 --> 00:07:04,480
It came at a heavy cost, but that

174
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:07,461
breakthrough saved a perimeter on the brink.

175
00:07:07,861 --> 00:07:11,472
U.S. records list UN losses at 51 killed,

176
00:07:11,492 --> 00:07:15,068
250 wounded, and 42 missing.

177
00:07:15,164 --> 00:07:16,897
Chinese casualties are estimated

178
00:07:16,917 --> 00:07:19,401
at about 5,000, though Chinese

179
00:07:19,421 --> 00:07:21,481
sources give just over 900.

180
00:07:21,916 --> 00:07:23,439
For the first time since entering

181
00:07:23,459 --> 00:07:25,055
the war, the Chinese had

182
00:07:25,075 --> 00:07:27,035
clearly been beaten in battle.

183
00:07:27,134 --> 00:07:29,151
Even the Chinese command later admitted

184
00:07:29,171 --> 00:07:31,321
it had underestimated its enemy.

185
00:07:31,740 --> 00:07:33,350
A week later, Ridgway struck

186
00:07:33,370 --> 00:07:35,137
back with Operation Killer.

187
00:07:35,192 --> 00:07:38,143
By March, Seoul was retaken, and the UN

188
00:07:38,163 --> 00:07:40,603
pushed back up to the 38th Parallel.

189
00:07:41,041 --> 00:07:43,824
Today, Chipyong-ni is home to

190
00:07:43,844 --> 00:07:45,747
a battle memorial hall and a

191
00:07:45,767 --> 00:07:47,771
monument to the French troops.

192
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:50,654
Every February, ceremonies here honor the

193
00:07:50,674 --> 00:07:53,382
victory and the sacrifices of those days.

194
00:07:53,818 --> 00:07:56,754
A small circle, just a mile across,

195
00:07:56,774 --> 00:07:58,916
drawn in a snowy basin.

196
00:07:58,955 --> 00:08:02,489
That circle changed the course of the war.

197
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:04,622
One surrounded ring.

198
00:08:04,677 --> 00:08:06,205
And those who held it to the end

199
00:08:06,225 --> 00:08:08,219
were the ones who would not step back,

200
00:08:08,239 --> 00:08:10,346
even with nowhere left to go.

201
00:08:10,793 --> 00:08:12,425
If this story meant something to you,

202
00:08:12,445 --> 00:08:14,871
please subscribe and like the video.

203
00:08:14,892 --> 00:08:16,022
We'll continue to bring the

204
00:08:16,042 --> 00:08:18,116
forgotten records of war to light.
